{"prompt": "I have a SPA that needs to make requests to an API. But the API only supports client_credentials authentication, so we use an intermediate gateway (Azure API Management) to authenticate with OAuth client_credentials and append the Bearer token to the header of the request. The API has rate limiting enabled. Explain whether the rate limit targets the Gateway, or if each user has separate rate limit quotas in this scenario", "response": "In your scenario, the rate limiting targets the Gateway, not each individual user. This is because the API Management Gateway is responsible for authenticating with OAuth client_credentials and appending the Bearer token to the header of the request on behalf of all users. The rate limiting is set at the Gateway level, and not at the user level, so all users are subject to the same rate limit quotas.

However, it's important to note that even though the rate limiting targets the Gateway, it's still possible for individual users to exceed their fair share of the allowed requests. This can happen if multiple users are making requests to the API at the same time, and one user is making significantly more requests than the others. In this case, the Gateway may still enforce the rate limiting, but the individual user who is making more requests than their fair share may still be impacted.

To avoid this, it's recommended to set up rate limiting at the user level as well, to ensure that each user is limited to their fair share of requests. This can be done by configuring the API Management Gateway to append the user's identity to the request header, and then setting up rate limiting at the user level in the API Management console. This will help ensure that all users are subject to the same rate limit quotas, and that no one user is able to exceed their fair share of requests."}
